strobe-photo-ask = How can a stroboscopic photo tell how the ball moved?
strobe-photo-title = A stroboscopic photo: distances and speed
strobe-photo-question = The ball of lab work 1 was photographed in the dark under a stroboscope: it flashes four times a second, and each flash leaves an image of the ball on the photo. The photo is 1 : 5 — five times smaller than life. The first photo: the ball rolls along the table, down from the groove; the second: it rolls down the groove from rest. We put a ruler to it and measure the gaps between the images. How can a stroboscopic photo tell how the ball moved?
strobe-photo-choices = By the gaps between the images: equal ones — uniform motion; and the real distance over the time between flashes is the speed
strobe-photo-choices-2 = By the images’ size: the bigger, the faster the ball moved
strobe-photo-choices-3 = No way: a photo has no time, a stopwatch is needed
strobe-photo-answer-why = The stroboscope flashes at equal intervals — here every 0.25 s — and the photo keeps where the ball was at each of those instants. So the gap between neighbouring images is the path in 0.25 s. The photo is 5 times smaller: what the ruler gives times 5 is the real distance; divided by 0.25 s — the speed. On the table the gaps are equal: the ball moved uniformly. In the groove they grow — about as 1 : 3 : 5 : 7 — the ball was speeding up, its average speed on each gap greater than on the one before. Distances on maps and plans are measured the same way: a scale of 1 : 100 000 means 1 cm on the map is 1 km on the ground.
